subcode:: sections
    $call section, www, $(code:output_www)
    $p
        $(code:output_www) lives in its own repository, $(a:github.com/hzhou/output_www,https://github.com/hzhou/output_www), and is installed the same way as $(code:output_c) ($(code:mydef_make), $(code:make), $(code:make install)). It provides three modules: $(code:module: www) for HTML pages, $(code:module: js) for JavaScript, and $(code:module: php) for PHP. This manual itself is written with $(code:output_www) -- see $(code:manual/mydef.def) in the $(code:MyDef) repository.
    $p
        HTML is a markup language. Most of its noise is closing tags, and indentation already shows the nesting, so the main thing $(code:output_www) adds is tags written as indented blocks.

    $call subsection, www_page, A first page
    &call codeprint, mydef
        page: hello
            module: www
            title: Hello

            $h1
                Hello, World!
            $p
                My first MyDef page.
    $p
        $(code:mydef_page hello.def) writes $(code:hello.html):
    &call codeprint, html
        <!doctype html>
        <html>
            <head>
                <meta charset="utf-8">
                <title>Hello</title>
            </head>
            <body>
                <h1>
                    Hello, World!
                </h1>
                <p>
                    My first MyDef page.
                </p>
            </body>
        </html>
    $p
        As with $(code:output_c), the page gets a default frame: $(code:basic_frame) from $(code:std_www.def) writes the $(code:&lt;html&gt;), $(code:&lt;head&gt;), and $(code:&lt;body&gt;) skeleton and puts the page code in the body. $(code:title:) sets the page title; without it, the title is the page name. Plain text and raw HTML lines are passed through as they are.

    $call subsection, www_tags, Tags
    $p
        A tag is written as $(code:$tagname) followed by a comma-separated list of attributes, with the content indented below it:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $p hint, #intro
            A paragraph with class and id.
        $a href:https://example.com, target:_blank
            a link
    &call codeprint, html
        <p class="hint" id="intro">
            A paragraph with class and id.
        </p>
        <a href="https://example.com" target="_blank">
            a link
        </a>
    $p
        Each item in the attribute list is read by its form:
    $table ref
        $tr
            $th "Form"
            $th "Meaning"
            $th "Example"
        $tr
            $td "bare word"
            $td "CSS class"
            $td
                $(code:$div note) → $(code:class="note")
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&num;name)
            $td "id"
            $td
                $(code:$div &num;box) → $(code:id="box")
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:key:value)
            $td "attribute"
            $td
                $(code:$img src:logo.png)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:"text")
            $td "short content on the same line"
            $td
                $(code:$li "one") → $(code:&lt;li&gt;one&lt;/li&gt;)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:/)
            $td "empty element"
            $td
                $(code:$div &num;box&#44; /) → $(code:&lt;div id="box"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;)
    $p
        $(code:$img) and $(code:$input) are written as self-closing tags. $(code:$input) gets $(code:type="text") unless a type is given, and its quoted text becomes the $(code:placeholder).
    $p
        Tags known by name are the common ones: $(code:html head body h1-h5 p pre div span form label fieldset button input textarea select option table tr td th ol ul li img a center b style). Any other tag is written with $(code:$tag): $(code:$tag nav&#44; main-nav) gives $(code:&lt;nav class="main-nav"&gt;).
    $p
        The attribute list is split at commas, and text in quotes is read as a $(code:key:value) attribute if it contains a colon. When content has commas or colons -- including content produced by a macro -- put it in the indented block instead of quotes:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $td "Note: see below"       &num; wrong: read as an attribute "Note"
        $td
            Note: see below         &num; right

    $call subsection, www_css, CSS
    $p
        $(code:CSS:) lines add style rules. They can appear anywhere in the page, typically next to the elements they style, and are all gathered into one $(code:&lt;style&gt;) block in the head. Rules for the same selector are merged, and a $(code:.css) file name becomes a stylesheet link:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $p hint
            CSS: .hint {color: #888}
            CSS: .hint {font-size: small}
            styled text
        CSS: theme.css
    &call codeprint, html
        <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="theme.css" />
        <style>
            .hint {color: #888; font-size: small}
        </style>
    $p
        One catch: $(code:CSS: ...) has the form of a page attribute, so $(code:CSS:) lines placed right below the $(code:page:) line, before any other code, are taken as page attributes and silently lost (see $(a:Collisions with target-language syntax,#syntax_clash)). Put them after the first line of content, or collect them in a subcode and $(code:$call) it, as this manual does.

    $call subsection, www_js, JavaScript
    $p
        A $(code:$script) block switches to JavaScript. Inside it, the code is handled by $(code:output_js), which gives JavaScript the same conveniences as the other modules -- optional semicolons, and $(code:$if), $(code:$for), $(code:$while), and $(code:$function) without parentheses and braces:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $script
            var n = 3
            $if n > 2
                console.log("big")
    &call codeprint, html
        <script type="text/javascript">
            var n = 3;
            if(n > 2){
                console.log("big");
            }
        </script>
    $p
        For pages with real scripting, use $(code:js_frame). It adds a $(code:&lt;script&gt;) block to the head and runs the $(code:js_onload) subcode when the page loads. Code in $(code:jscode:) subcodes is JavaScript, and $(code:$global) declares a global variable at the top of the script:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        page: counter, js_frame
            module: www
            title: Counter
            $button &num;btn, "Click me"
            $span &num;count, "0"

        jscode:: js_onload
            $global clicks = 0
            &call on_click, btn
                clicks++
                $(id:count).innerHTML = clicks
    &call codeprint, html
        <script type="text/javascript">
            var clicks = 0;
            window.onload = function(){
                document.getElementById("btn").addEventListener("click", function(event){
                    clicks++;
                    document.getElementById('count').innerHTML = clicks;
                }, false);
            };
        </script>
    $p
        $(code:std_js.def) provides these helpers. The macros $(code:&dollar;(id:x)), $(code:&dollar;(value:x)), $(code:&dollar;(style:x)), $(code:&dollar;(show:x)), and $(code:&dollar;(hide:x)) are short for $(code:document.getElementById) expressions. The block subcodes $(code:on_click), $(code:on_change), $(code:setf), $(code:ajax_get), and $(code:ajax_post) wrap the usual event and request patterns. $(code:js_frame) also calls the optional subcodes $(code:js_global) and $(code:css_global).
    $p
        A page with $(code:module: js) produces a standalone $(code:.js) file. $(code:$global) works there too: the $(code:var) declarations go at the top of the file.

    $call subsection, www_php, PHP
    $p
        PHP support is simpler and less developed. A page with $(code:type: php) is written as a $(code:.php) file, and in its HTML text, a $(code:$var) becomes $(code:&lt;?php echo $var ?&gt;):
    &call codeprint, mydef
        page: greet
            module: www
            type: php
            $h1
                Hello, $name!
    &call codeprint, html
        <h1>
            Hello, <?php echo $name ?>!
        </h1>
    $p
        For a page that is all PHP, use $(code:php_frame), which compiles the page code as PHP; tags in PHP code are written out with $(code:echo). $(code:phpcode:) subcodes are also PHP, and can be called from an HTML page: the output switches into $(code:&lt;?php ... ?&gt;) for the call and back to HTML after it. $(code:$form) adds $(code:method="POST") when no method is given; without an $(code:action), the form submits to the current URL.

    $call subsection, www_std, $(code:std_html.def) and other libraries
    $p
        $(code:std_www.def) is loaded automatically and brings in $(code:std_html.def), $(code:std_js.def), and $(code:std_php.def). $(code:std_html.def) has inline macros for the most common markup:
    $table ref
        $tr
            $th "Macro"
            $th "Output"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(a:text&#44;url))
            $td
                $(code:&lt;a href="url"&gt;text&lt;/a&gt;)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(url:u))
            $td
                $(code:&lt;a href="u"&gt;u&lt;/a&gt;)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(anchor:name))
            $td
                $(code:&lt;a name="name"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(code:text))
            $td
                $(code:&lt;code&gt;text&lt;/code&gt;)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(b:text)) / $(code:&dollar;(em:text))
            $td
                $(code:&lt;strong&gt;) / $(code:&lt;em&gt;)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(img:src))
            $td
                $(code:&lt;img src="src" /&gt;)
    $p
        It also has form helpers ($(code:input_text), $(code:input_pass), $(code:input_checkbox), $(code:input_radio), $(code:input_hidden), $(code:input_submit), $(code:input_option)) and $(code:js_src) for loading a script file:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        $call js_src, app.js
        $form action:save.php
            $call input_text, username
            $call input_checkbox, remember, Remember me
            $call input_submit, Save
    $p
        Two optional libraries are included with $(code:include:). $(code:html/code.def) provides $(code:codeprint), which formats code listings (with highlighting for $(code:mydef) code) -- every code example in this manual goes through it:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        include: html/code.def

        page: t
            module: www
            $call code_css
            &call codeprint, mydef
                page: t
                    $print Hello
    $p
        $(code:html/markdown.def) converts a block of Markdown to HTML, using an external $(code:markdown_translate.pl) script.
